{"about":"0b14385d18dbb54744c46bd88d961c2cf563cc0e20d78e2419a4362748cd7e4b","body":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms if understood not as an inductive prediction of future success, but as a methodological rule for critical rationality. The argument holds that while corroboration reports only past performance, choosing the theory that has withstood the severest tests is the only rational strategy available to an agent who seeks truth and rejects dogmatism. This choice does not smuggle in induction because it makes no probabilistic claim about the future; rather, it acknowledges that the unrefuted theory is currently the best candidate for truth, whereas the refuted one is known to be false. The surviving case is that this preference is a non-inductive, pragmatic maxim: we act on the best-tested theory because it is the only one that has not yet failed, not because we believe it will succeed. The cost of accepting this view is the abandonment of any assurance that science converges on truth or that practical action based on scientific theories is likely to succeed; it requires scientists to accept that their 'rational' choices may still lead to catastrophic failure, rendering rationality a matter of procedural integrity rather than instrumental reliability.","kind":"mini.commitment-proposal.v1","named":"The Defensibility of Corroboration as Rational Choice Without Induction"}